# `M154g` step 2b / `C67`, `C70`, `C71`, `C72` — the known-answer plant for four of the six step
# keywords the whole suite is written in.
#
# **This file is meant to pass.** Its siblings `hard-stop-semantics.tflw` (`C68`, `C69`) carry the
# two claims that can only be shown by a step going red.
#
# ## Why the most-used constructs in the language were the least proved
#
# `api` appears 1139 times in this repository, `expect` 1692, `capture` 523. Not one of those uses
# is evidence for the construct itself, and the reason is the same every time: they are all
# *instruments*, pointed at something else. A test that creates a product and asserts its price is
# evidence about the price. It says nothing about `api` that would change if `api` fired the request
# twice, and nothing about `capture` that would change if `capture` re-read the response lazily
# instead of binding it — because in the ordinary shape of a test, nothing happens between the
# capture and its use.
#
# So the claims below are not "this keyword works". They are the specific, checkable sentences
# `tflw spec --json` states about each one, each paired with the wrong implementation it excludes:
#
#   `api`      "issue **one** HTTP request"          vs. a step that fires twice, or retries silently
#   `let`      "bind a value … for later steps"      vs. a name re-evaluated at each interpolation
#   `log`      "into the run log **and the report**" vs. a line that only ever reached stdout
#   `wait`     "**re-issue** … until it passes"      vs. a single request with a generous timeout
#
# ## The observable is the server's own counter, not the test's verdict
#
# Every claim here is settled by `apiV2/src/lifecycle/`, the same counter module `C4` and `C5` use:
# `POST /v1/lifecycle/mark` returns the number of times that label has been marked, and
# `GET /v1/lifecycle/counts` lets the grader assert the **complement** — that nothing was marked
# beyond what this file declares. That matters more here than anywhere else in the roster, because
# the failure mode being excluded for `api` is an *extra* request, and an extra request is invisible
# to every assertion that only looks at the last response.
#
# `POST /v1/lifecycle/attempt` answers 503 until a per-key attempt number and 200 from then on;
# `c72settles` is configured to settle on attempt **3**, which is `C72`'s whole known answer.

before file
  # Fixed labels need a clean slate, exactly as `C4`'s plant does — the grader reads these counters
  # back by name, so a leftover count from a previous run would read as a duplicate request.
  api POST /lifecycle/reset
  expect status equals 200

@constructs @workhorses
test "one `api` step issues exactly one request, and the server is the one that says so"
  # The response carries the server's own arrival count for this label, so the first request
  # asserting `1` is not a tautology: an `api` that fired twice would return `2` here, and an `api`
  # that silently retried an idempotent-looking POST would return more.
  api POST /lifecycle/mark body { label: "c67once" }
  expect status equals 200
  expect body.count equals 1

  # And the counter really is live — without this second step, `count: 1` is equally consistent with
  # a server that always answers `1`, which would make the assertion above prove nothing.
  api POST /lifecycle/mark body { label: "c67once" }
  expect status equals 200
  expect body.count equals 2

@constructs @workhorses
test "`let` binds its value once — two interpolations of one name are the same value"
  # `random string 8` is the point. A literal would pass this test under either implementation, so
  # it could not tell "bind, then interpolate the binding" from "re-evaluate the expression at each
  # `{tag}`". Under the second reading the two marks below carry *different* labels and both come
  # back `count: 1`.
  let tag = random string 8
  api POST /lifecycle/mark body { label: "c70-{tag}" }
  expect status equals 200
  expect body.count equals 1

  api POST /lifecycle/mark body { label: "c70-{tag}" }
  expect status equals 200
  expect body.count equals 2

@constructs @workhorses
test "`log` writes one authored line into the report, interpolated, at the level it was given"
  # Nothing in this test can assert the log line — `log` is not an assertion and never fails, the
  # same shape as `screenshot` in `C41`. The known answer is read out of `report/results.json` by
  # the grader: one `log` step, level `warn`, and the message with `{subject}` **resolved**. A `log`
  # that reached stdout and not the report leaves nothing there to find; one that stored the raw
  # source line leaves `{subject}` unexpanded.
  api POST /lifecycle/mark body { label: "c71logged" }
  expect status equals 200
  capture body.label as subject
  log warn "c71 observed {subject}"

@constructs @workhorses
test "`wait until api` re-issues the request until its condition holds, then stops"
  # `c72settles` answers 503 on attempts 1 and 2 and 200 on attempt 3. A `wait` that issued a single
  # request would see `settled: false` and time out; the assertion after the block pins the exact
  # attempt the successful response came from, so "it re-issued" is a number and not an inference.
  wait until api POST /lifecycle/attempt body { key: "c72settles" }
    expect body.settled equals true
  expect body.attempt equals 3

  # The other half, and the one a passing wait cannot show on its own: it stopped. The grader reads
  # `attempts.c72settles` back and requires exactly 3 — a `wait` that kept polling out its budget
  # after the condition held would leave a larger number here and still be green.
  api GET /lifecycle/counts
  expect status equals 200
  expect body.attempts.c72settles equals 3
